(a) Definitions. The following words, terms and phrases, when used in this section, shall have the following meaning, except where the context clearly indicates a different meaning:
Commercial property means any property used for retail, service, office, or mixed-use commercial purposes and open to the public.
Convenience goods means basic food, household and/or pharmaceutical items.
Convenience store means any business that is primarily engaged in the retail sale of convenience goods, or both convenience goods and gasoline, and has less than 10,000 square feet of retail floor space. Convenience store does not include any business where there is no retail floor space accessible to the public.
Obstructive loitering means loitering that interferes with ingress, egress, or free passage of persons or vehicles.
Public right-of-way means any street, median, or other area dedicated to public use.
Repeated loitering means loitering by the same individual or group occurring on two or more occasions within a 24-hour period after a warning has been issued.
(b) General prohibition. It shall be unlawful for any person to loiter on:
(1) Any public property;
(2) Any commercial property open to the public;
(3) Any parking lot, fuel station, or shopping center;
(4) Any public right-of-way;
in a manner that:
• Obstructs pedestrian or vehicular movement;
• Interferes with lawful business activity;
• Creates a risk to public safety; or
• Constitutes repeated loitering after warning.
(c) Condoning of loitering prohibited. Owners, managers, and/or operators of any commercial property, including but not limited to convenience stores, shall not knowingly permit loitering and shall take reasonable steps to disperse such activity.
It shall be unlawful for the owner, manager and/or operator of any commercial property to allow any person to loiter on or about the licensed premises, without taking prompt action to cause such loiterer to be removed from the premises. The unlawful loitering of a person at a commercial property for a period of more than 30 minutes shall constitute prima facie evidence that the owner, manager and/or operator of the establishment is unlawfully condoning such prohibited conduct.
(d) Duty to act—Property owners. All commercial property owners and operators shall:
(1) Post "No Loitering" signage in visible locations;
(2) Contact law enforcement when individuals refuse to disperse;
(3) Maintain premises free from persistent nuisance activity.
Failure to take reasonable action after notice shall constitute a violation.
(e) Prominent display of loitering prohibition in convenience stores. Convenience stores within the City, pursuant to this chapter, shall cause the following language to be prominently displayed, in no less than 48-inch font, in a manner which is visible to the general public on the exterior and interior of the establishment:
Loitering Prohibited:
This establishment is required to prohibit loitering, pursuant to Title 15, Sec. 15-4005 of the South Fulton Code of Ordinances.
(f) Prohibited conduct. In addition to general loitering, the following are expressly prohibited:
(1) Loitering that blocks entrances, exits, or ADA access routes;
(2) Loitering in or around vehicles for the purpose of solicitation or unlawful activity;
(3) Loitering combined with:
• Panhandling;
• Public intoxication;
• Drug-related activity.
(4) Congregating in a manner that causes repeated complaints or calls for service.
(g) Warning and enforcement protocol.
(1) A law enforcement officer shall issue a verbal warning prior to citation unless immediate enforcement is necessary.
(2) Continued violation after warning shall result in citation or arrest as authorized by law.
(3) Repeated violations may result in trespass enforcement.
(h) Enforcement documentation requirement. The South Fulton Police Department shall:
(1) Document all loitering-related calls for service;
(2) Record warnings, citations, and enforcement actions;
(3) Maintain centralized reporting data for compliance review.
(i) Public nuisance designation. Any location with repeated violations of this section shall be subject to designation as a public nuisance property, triggering:
(1) Code enforcement action;
(2) Administrative penalties;
(3) Abatement procedures.
(j) Penalties. Violations shall be punishable as provided in Section [General Penalty Clause Placeholder].
Repeated violations may result in enhanced penalties and/or trespass enforcement.
Notice to convenience stores selling alcohol. In addition to other penalties and sanctions authorized by state law and the City Code, the violation of this chapter by owners, managers and/or operators of convenience stores shall subject the convenience store to termination, revocation and/or suspension of its license to sell alcoholic beverages.
(Ord. No. 2019-003, § 1, 1-22-2019; Ord. No. 2026-021, § 1, 7-14-2026)

