1288:
(a) Purpose. This section establishes content-neutral regulations governing camping on public property and certain private property open to the public in order to:
(1) Protect public health and safety;
(2) Maintain access to sidewalks, rights-of-way, and commercial areas;
(3) Prevent obstruction and nuisance conditions;
(4) Ensure consistent enforcement and documentation.
(b) Definitions. For purposes of this section:
(1) Camping means residing in or using public or private property for living accommodation purposes, including but not limited to:
a. Sleeping or preparing to sleep overnight;
b. Using tents, tarps, or temporary shelters;
c. Storing personal belongings in a manner consistent with habitation;
d. Using bedding, sleeping bags, or mattresses outdoors.
(2) Public property means any property owned, leased, or controlled by the City, including but not limited to:
a. Parks;
b. Sidewalks;
c. Streets and medians;
d. Rights-of-way;
e. Public buildings and grounds.
(3) Publicly accessible private property means private property open to the public for commercial or business purposes.
(4) Obstruction means blocking or interfering with the free passage of pedestrians, vehicles, or access to property.
(c) Prohibited conduct. It shall be unlawful for any person to engage in camping:
(1) On any public property, including sidewalks, medians, and rights-of-way;
(2) On publicly accessible private property without the consent of the property owner;
(3) In a manner that:
a. Obstructs pedestrian or vehicular traffic;
b. Interferes with ingress or egress to a business or residence;
c. Creates a health or sanitation hazard;
d. Damages public or private property.
(d) Time, place, and manner limitations. This section is intended to regulate the time, place, and manner of camping and shall not be interpreted to prohibit lawful presence in public spaces during normal use.
(e) Notice and opportunity to comply.
(1) Prior to enforcement, a law enforcement officer or code enforcement officer shall provide a verbal notice directing the individual to cease the prohibited conduct and remove personal belongings.
(2) If the violation continues, the officer may issue a citation or take enforcement action.
(3) Written notice may be posted at locations where recurring violations occur.
(4) Campers who claim a right to remain on private property shall be subject to enforcement of the provisions of the Georgia Unlawful Squatters law O.C.G.A. § 16-7-21.1.
(f) Removal of personal property.
(1) Personal property that is unattended or abandoned in violation of this section may be removed by the city.
(2) The city shall make reasonable efforts to:
a. Identify and separate personal property from trash;
b. Avoid destruction of identifiable personal items.
(g) Storage and retrieval of property.
(1) Non-hazardous non-perishable personal property removed pursuant to this section shall be stored for a minimum of 30 days and a maximum of 60 days.
(2) The City shall provide reasonable means for individuals to retrieve stored property upon request.
(3) Perishable, hazardous, or contaminated items should be discarded.
(4) Illegal substances or items should be referred to law enforcement.
(h) Enforcement authority. This section may be enforced by:
(1) The South Fulton Police Department;
(2) Code enforcement officers;
(3) Other authorized city officials.
Campers who claim a right to remain on private property shall be subject to enforcement of the provisions of the Georgia Unlawful Squatters law O.C.G.A. § 16-7-21.1.
(i) Documentation and reporting (HB 295 Compliance Provision). The city shall document all enforcement actions under this section, including:
(1) Calls for service;
(2) Warnings issued;
(3) Citations issued;
(4) Property removal actions;
(5) Locations of recurring activity.
Such records shall be maintained in a centralized system and made available for administrative review and legal compliance.
(j) Public nuisance designation. Repeated violations at a specific location may result in designation as a public nuisance property, subject to additional enforcement and abatement procedures under applicable city ordinances.
(k) Coordination with services. Where practicable, enforcement personnel are encouraged to provide information regarding available social services, shelters, or outreach programs prior to or during enforcement.
(l) Penalties. Any person found in violation of this section shall be subject to:
(1) Citation;
(2) Fines as provided in the city's general penalty provisions;
(3) Additional enforcement actions as permitted by law.
(m) Exceptions. This section shall not apply to:
(1) Lawful camping in designated areas expressly authorized by the city or property owner;
(2) Emergency situations where temporary shelter is necessary for safety;
(3) Permitted events or activities authorized by the city or property owner.
(Ord. No. 2026-019, § 1, 7-14-2026)

